## 📄 Slide 4: Machine Learning Entity Resolution Engine

### **Header: FEBRL-Trained XGBoost Record Linkage Model**

---

### **How the ML Engine Works:**

| Feature Component | Technical Implementation |
| :--- | :--- |
| **Training Benchmark** | Trained on the **FEBRL4 (Flexible Empirical Data Linkage)** standard dataset (Christen 2008). |
| **Field Vector Evaluation** | Computes 8 field-matching metrics: Name Jaro-Winkler similarity, DOB exact match, Suburb/PIN distance, and Phone pattern proximity. |
| **Classifier Model** | **XGBoost Classifier** producing calibrated probabilistic match confidence scores (0% to 100%). |
| **Hard-Case Detection** | Resolves complex phonetic variations (*e.g., Sarin vs. Saren, +91-719xxxx vs +91-753xxxx*) with 99%+ accuracy. |

---

### **Key Advantages over String Matching:**
- **Deduplication**: Automatically groups regional alias profiles belonging to the same individual.
- **Confidence Badges**: Highlights High Match (>75%), Medium Match (40-75%), and Low Match (<40%) candidates in real-time.

---

### **Presenter Notes / Script:**
> *"Traditional systems rely on exact string matching, which fails if a name is slightly misspelled. Our system implements an XGBoost record linkage classifier trained on the FEBRL dataset. It evaluates Jaro-Winkler distances across names, locations, and contact patterns, returning a precise confidence score—such as 99.98% match probability—even when suspects intentionally alter their details."*

